---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/calculus.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';
---
<Base wide title="Calculus in motion" description="Learn derivatives by moving, stretching and perturbing mathematical objects. Nine interactive experiments connect slopes, logs, partial derivatives, gradients, curvature and accumulation." pageClass="calculus-page">
 <div class="cl-shell">
  <header class="cl-header"><p class="cl-eyebrow"><a href="/teaching">Teaching</a> / Interactive mathematics</p><div><h1>Calculus in <em>motion.</em></h1><p>Derivatives, logs,<br/>and functions of several variables.</p></div></header>
  <section data-calculus aria-label="Interactive calculus experiments"><p>Loading the experiments…</p><noscript><p>These experiments need JavaScript. They cover derivatives as local change, the product and chain rules, logarithms, partial derivatives, gradients, Taylor approximations, optimization, and the fundamental theorem of calculus. Read the freely accessible references below for the underlying mathematics.</p></noscript></section>
  <footer class="cl-references"><div><h2>References</h2><p>Inspired by <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B1J6Ou4q8vE">Alan Becker’s <em>Animation vs. Math</em></a>. </p><p><a href="/macroeconomics/">Macroeconomics ↗</a></p></div><div><h3>Definitions &amp; further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-1-defining-the-derivative">OpenStax: defining the derivative</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-9-derivatives-of-exponential-and-logarithmic-functions">Exponential and logarithmic derivatives</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-5-the-chain-rule">Multivariable chain rule</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-6-directional-derivatives-and-the-gradient">Directional derivatives and gradients</a></li></ul></div></footer>
 </div>
</Base>
<script>import '../scripts/calculus-lab.mjs';</script>
